Cloud Infrastructure Components
1. Compute Resources
Purpose

Compute resources supply the processing capability required to execute applications, commands, and services. These resources primarily consist of the computer's CPU and memory (RAM).

Importance in Cloud Computing

Compute resources are essential because applications require processing power to perform different operations. With cloud computing, organizations can scale their computing resources up or down based on workload requirements without needing to buy additional physical servers.

KillerCoda Environment

The KillerCoda environment provides 1 vCPU powered by an Intel Xeon E312xx (Sandy Bridge, IBRS update) processor and approximately 1.96 GiB of RAM. These resources enable the Linux virtual machine to execute commands and operate applications.

2. Storage Resources
Purpose

Storage resources provide the necessary space for storing the operating system, applications, files, and other types of data.

Importance in Cloud Computing

Storage is essential because cloud applications require a dependable location for storing and accessing information. Cloud storage also enables organizations to maintain large amounts of data without relying completely on physical storage equipment.

KillerCoda Environment

The KillerCoda environment includes a 19 GiB root filesystem located at /dev/vda1. Approximately 5.4 GiB is currently used, while around 13 GiB remains available. The environment also contains other mounted filesystems, including /dev/vda15 and /dev/vda16.

3. Networking Resources
Purpose

Networking resources enable computers, servers, applications, and other devices to exchange information and communicate with one another.

Importance in Cloud Computing

Networking is essential in cloud computing because services must communicate with users, applications, databases, and other cloud resources. A properly configured network also manages communication between resources and provides connectivity to cloud-based services.

KillerCoda Environment

The KillerCoda server uses a primary network interface named enp1s0, which has the IP address 172.30.1.2/24. It also includes a Docker bridge interface named docker0, using the address 172.17.0.1/16. These interfaces allow the virtual machine and its services to communicate within the network environment.

4. Operating System
Purpose

An operating system controls and manages computer hardware while providing the environment required for applications and commands to operate.

Importance in Cloud Computing

The operating system plays an important role in managing resources such as CPU, memory, storage, and networking. It also provides the tools and working environment that cloud engineers use to administer and maintain servers.

KillerCoda Environment

The KillerCoda environment runs an Ubuntu-based Linux virtual machine with kernel version 6.8.0-136-generic. The specific Ubuntu version could not be identified because the /etc/os-release file was unavailable. Linux provides the command-line environment used to examine and manage the cloud server.

Relationship Between the Components

These four components work together to create a functional cloud environment. The operating system manages the available compute, storage, and networking resources. Compute resources handle application processing, storage resources maintain files and data, and networking resources enable communication between the server and its services. In the KillerCoda environment, all of these components operate together within a virtual machine to provide a basic cloud computing environment.
